⏺ User answered Claude's questions:
  ⎿  · K-threat selection (requirement 3): union of nearest-sensed and highest 
     observable-risk threats. What should drive "observable risk" ranking, given
     blue only has pd/lock/range/envelope radius for sensed or remembered 
     threats? → Lock x inverse range-to-envelope (Recommended)
     · Requirement 2 says add last-known-track uncertainty. Should 
     remembered-but-currently-unsensed threats keep reporting live ground-truth 
     position/velocity (as now), or freeze to the last-sensed snapshot with 
     growing uncertainty? → Freeze to last-sensed snapshot (Recommended)
     · Requirement 7 (ablation protocol): what should it concretely produce? → A
     runnable eval script + checklist doc (Recommended)

  Searched for 1 pattern (ctrl+o to expand)

⏺ Design, based on repo exploration (naigos/env/obs.py, threats.py,
  spatial_hash.py, rl/networks.py, rl/ppo.py, rl/checkpoint.py,
  env/flight_env.py):

  1. Observation: vz, closure, track age/uncertainty

  Add new EnvState per-(threat,blue) fields, updated each step() alongside
  existing lock/dwell:
  - track_pos, track_vel (T,B,3): snapshot of threat position/velocity frozen at
    the last step it was actually sensed (sensed_mask true), held constant
    otherwise. This fixes an existing leak — "remembered" (locked-but-unsensed)
    threats currently read live ground-truth position/velocity from ThreatState,
    which is an oracle. Freezing to last-sensed makes the new uncertainty
    feature meaningful.
  - track_age (T,B): seconds since lock last rose from ~0 (track start), reset
    when lock decays below CONTACT_THRESHOLD.
  - stale_age (T,B): seconds since last sensed; 0 while currently sensed,
    increments otherwise.

  New threat_features columns, gated behind a new EnvConfig.obs_track_features: 
  bool = False flag (same pattern as obs_edge_features, so threat_feat_dim only
  grows when the flag is on and old checkpoints keep working unflagged):
  - vz component of tv_e (currently zeroed — table already has ThreatState.vz
    computed and unused in obs)
  - closure = -dot(unit(rel), vel_blue - track_vel) (purely observable, from
    ego-frame quantities)
  - track_age normalized
  - uncertainty = 1 - exp(-stale_age/tau) (0 when just sensed, saturating while
    stale)

  threat_feat_dim = 9 + n_kinds (off) or 13 + n_kinds (on).

  2. K-threat selection: deterministic union

  Split k_threat_obs into k_threat_near (nearest sensed, via existing
  spatial_hash.topk_neighbours) and k_threat_risk = k_threat_obs - k_threat_near
  (new field, default half). Risk score = lock * (1 - 
  clip(range_to_track/lethal_range, 0, 1)) using track_pos/lock, computed over
  all T threats (brute-force — T is small, no spatial hash needed), masked to
  active & lock > CONTACT_THRESHOLD, and excluding whatever the nearest-sensed
  set already picked (via a taken boolean to avoid duplicates). Concatenate the
  two fixed-width index/mask blocks. Fully static-shape, jit/vmap-safe, no sort
  over dynamic sets.

  3. Recurrent actor

  Actor gains recurrent: bool, gru_hidden: int. After the existing trunk MLP, an
  nn.GRUCell updates a per-agent hidden state (n_blue, gru_hidden) before the
  policy head. Actor.apply always takes/returns a hidden_state argument (width 0
  when recurrent=False, a pure passthrough — no branching needed by callers,
  and old feed-forward checkpoints' param pytree is structurally unaffected
  since no GRU submodule exists when the flag is off).
  recurrent_actor/gru_hidden move onto PPOConfig (already serialized whole into
  checkpoints) and get mirrored at the checkpoint's top level (next to
  obs_edge_features in env_cfg) so demo/replay.py and demo/live.py can
  reconstruct the right Actor without needing the recovery block.

  4. Hidden-state threading

  One calling convention everywhere: policy(obs, hidden, key) -> (action, 
  new_hidden). greedy_policy and the PPO sampling policy both adopt it.
  - ppo.collect's per-world scan carries hidden alongside (state, obs, key);
    zeroed on the existing whole-world auto-reset (jnp.where(done, ...), same
    mechanism already used for state/obs).
  - env.rollout (used by evaluate_route_bank, replay, verifier) gains a hidden
    carry the same way.
  - env.respawn (continuous per-agent re-tasking in live.py) zeros exactly the
    respawned agents' hidden rows, mirroring how it already clears lock/dwell
    columns via the mask.
  - demo/replay.py/demo/live.py's Python-level frame loops thread a local hidden
    variable across iterations, resetting at episode start and at respawn.

  Resume: restore_learner's existing structural _graft check already refuses a
  mismatched GRU-vs-no-GRU pytree. I'll add a friendlier upfront message (same
  style as curriculum_compatibility) comparing the checkpoint's recorded
  recurrent_actor/gru_hidden against the current PPOConfig before the low-level
  graft error fires.

  5. Tests

  New/extended cases: K-selection masking + dedup correctness, permutation
  invariance (shuffled threat slots, shuffled agent order) with the new features
  and with recurrent=True, hidden-state zeroing on respawn and on world
  auto-reset, a temporary-terrain-masking scenario asserting track_pos freezes
  and uncertainty rises monotonically then resets on re-acquisition, JIT/vmap
  compilation of the recurrent path end-to-end, and an extension of
  test_invariant.py's isolation check — permuting agents (including their hidden
  rows) must permute the actions identically, proving the GRU still operates
  strictly per-agent with no cross-agent or centralized leakage.

  6. Ablation protocol

  New naigos/rl/ablation.py: builds two EnvConfig variants (threat spawn_weight
  biased near-exclusively toward mobile_ground vs. interceptor), loads a
  feed-forward and a recurrent checkpoint, runs matched fixed-seed batched
  rollouts through each, and reports shootdown/arrival/survival rate deltas per
  config. docs/ablation-protocol.md documents how to produce the matched pair of
  checkpoints (same seed/curriculum, only ppo.recurrent_actor differing) and
  run the comparison.

  Does this design look right before I write it up as the spec?

